  "question": {
    "text": "Let $f: \\mathbf{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbf{R}$ be a function given by\n$f(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\frac{1-\\cos 2 x}{x^2}, & x < 0 \\\\\n\\alpha, & x=0, \\\\\n\\frac{\\beta \\sqrt{1-\\cos x}}{x}, & x>0\n\\end{array}\\right.$\nwhere $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbf{R}$. If $f$ is continuous at $x=0$, then $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2$ is equal to :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "3",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "12",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "48",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "6",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & f\\left(0^{-}\\right)=\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0^{-}} \\frac{2 \\sin ^2 x}{x^2}=2=\\alpha \\\\ & f\\left(0^{+}\\right)=\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0^{+}} \\beta \\times \\sqrt{2} \\frac{\\sin \\frac{x}{2}}{2 \\frac{x}{2}}=\\frac{\\beta}{\\sqrt{2}}=2 \\\\ & \\Rightarrow \\beta=2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ & \\alpha^2+\\beta^2=4+8=12\\end{aligned}$"
  }
}